	<h1>Libraries — npm</h1>
	<p>Two packages I use in production. smart-enums is older than Homeroll; ioc-manifest came out of it.</p>

	<h2>smart-enums</h2>
	<p>
		One declarative place for every constant in a codebase, with derived values and behavior attached
		to each. I've been sneaking this idea into every job since my C# days, usually failing or
		regretting it. Homeroll is where I finally used it to the breaking point and got it right.
		<a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@reharik/smart-enum">npm</a>
		<a href="/blog/2026-08-your-status-enum-lives-in-five-files/">post</a>
	</p>

	<h2>ioc-manifest</h2>
	<p>
		Build-time dependency injection for TypeScript. It reads your factories and classes, checks the
		wiring, and generates a typed Awilix container, so nothing is registered by hand and nothing is
		scanned at runtime. Loosely inspired by StructureMap's conventions, which I'd used in C# and
		missed.
		<a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/ioc-manifest">npm</a>
		<a href="/blog/2026-10-20-i-argued-ioc-was-pointless/">post</a>
	</p>

	<p>
		Posts live at <a href="/blog">cannibalcode.com</a> and also run on
		<a href="https://medium.com/@harik.raif">Medium</a> and <a href="https://dev.to/reharik">dev.to</a>.
